# State Management Guidelines

## Scope / Flexibility Notice
These guidelines apply to application state management with preference for BLoC and Riverpod patterns. Choose the appropriate pattern based on feature complexity and team familiarity.

## Best Practices
- Keep presentation layer free of business logic
- Maintain unidirectional data flow (state → UI → events → state)
- Design states as immutable objects with equality comparisons
- Minimize state rebuilds by using selective state consumption
- Separate UI rendering logic from state management code
- Use extension methods to add domain-specific functionality to state objects

## File / Project Structure Guidelines
- For BLoC pattern:
  - Place bloc files in `lib/presentation/blocs/` or feature-specific directories
  - Follow naming convention: `feature_bloc.dart`, `feature_event.dart`, `feature_state.dart`
  - Group related BLoCs in subdirectories by feature
- For Riverpod:
  - Place providers in `lib/presentation/providers/` or feature-specific directories
  - Follow naming convention: `feature_provider.dart`, `feature_state.dart`
  - Keep provider definitions separate from their implementations

## Coding & Styling Conventions
- Use sealed classes/enums for events in BLoC pattern
- Implement copyWith methods for all state classes
- Make state classes @immutable
- Prefer state extension methods over helper methods
- Use freezed or equatable for state equality
- Keep BLoC/provider files focused on a single responsibility
- Use descriptive state class names: `LoadingState`, `LoadedState`, `ErrorState`

## Performance & Optimization Tips
- Implement equality comparison for all state classes to prevent unnecessary rebuilds
- Use selective state consumption (BlocSelector/select) to limit widget rebuilds
- Cache expensive computations within state objects
- Avoid deep object nesting in state to simplify equality checks
- Implement state combination strategically for related states

## Testing & Validation Guidelines
- Unit test all state transitions
- Mock dependencies in BLoC/provider tests
- Test initialization, events, and state transitions separately
- Verify correct state emissions using bloc_test
- Use Golden tests for complex widget state combinations